; The comma of a tuple takes terms. `reflexive((R), $A)` passes the operator `R` (a parameter, no
; statement) in a tuple; its first use made Kurt infer `prop , 1`, a statement place of the comma
; -- harmless until binders range over terms (2026-10-20): then every later `(x, x)` put `x` at a
; statement place, and `∀ x ∈ A (lt(x, x))` was refused ("`forall` binds `x`, a statement").
load logic, set
const A, lt
prop lt 0
prop reflexive 0
var R
prop R 0
def reflexive((R), $A) ⇔ ∀ x ∈ $A (R(x, x))
use ∀ x ∈ A (lt(x, x))
reflexive(lt, A)

; a statement in a tuple is no object
prop P, Q
expect "TypeError" "is a statement where a term is required"
    use (P, Q) = (Q, P)
